{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 5","reason":"Each condition says n+4 is divisible by the corresponding divisor. Since lcm(6,9,11) = 198, the three-digit solutions are 194, 392, 590, 788, and 986.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_8_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"primary","label":"Add4 to turn all three remainder conditions into divisibility","methodDirectNodeIds":{"primary":["math:simultaneous-congruences"]},"nodeIds":["math:simultaneous-congruences"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (9)","reason":"Modulo 9, concatenation gives N ≡ 1+2+...+44 = 990 ≡ 0. Its last digit gives N ≡ 4 modulo 5. The unique residue modulo 45 satisfying both conditions is 9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_10B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"split-moduli","label":"Combine remainders modulo 5 and 9","methodDirectNodeIds":{"split-moduli":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_h204"]},"nodeIds":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_h204"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"digit-sum-and-divisibility","label":"Use divisibility by 5 and 9","methodDirectNodeIds":{"digit-sum-and-divisibility":["math:modular-congruence","math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p154"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p154"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 9","reason":"Modulo 9, concatenation has the same residue as 1 + 2 + ... + 44 = 990, so N is 0 modulo 9. Its last digit gives N = 4 modulo 5. Of 0, 9, 18, 27, and 36, only 9 is 4 modulo 5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_12B_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"divisibility-by-five-and-nine","label":"Combine divisibility conditions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"divisibility-by-five-and-nine":["math:modular-congruence","math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p154"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p154"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 100 days","reason":"Within days 1 through 20, Al rests on 4,8,12,16,20 and Barb on 8,9,10,18,19,20. They share days 8 and 20. There are 1000/20=50 such periods, giving 100 shared rest-days.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1993_AMC_12_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_p038"],"id":"twenty-day-period","label":"Count within a shared repeating period","methodDirectNodeIds":{"twenty-day-period":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p159"]},"nodeIds":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p159","cnm_p153","cnm_p038"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 118","reason":"The conditions give n ≡ 9 (mod 20). The candidates are 9, 29, 49, 69, and 89; only 29 and 89 are prime, and their sum is 118.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1999_AMC_12_Q04","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_p159"],"id":"q04-repeating-blocks","label":"Find the repeating candidates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"q04-repeating-blocks":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p157"]},"nodeIds":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p157","cnm_p153","cnm_p159"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 60 and 79","reason":"The number minus 2 must be a positive multiple of lcm(3,4,5,6) = 60. The smallest number is 62, which lies between 60 and 79.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_8_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p050","cnm_p153"],"id":"subtract-common-remainder","label":"Remove the common remainder","methodDirectNodeIds":{"subtract-common-remainder":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p159"]},"nodeIds":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p159","cnm_p050","cnm_p153"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 0 coins","reason":"If the number is n, then n + 2 is divisible by both 6 and 5. Its smallest possible value is 30, giving n = 28, which leaves remainder 0 upon division by 7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2006_AMC_8_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p050","cnm_p153"],"id":"add_two","label":"Add two coins to complete both divisions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"add_two":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p159","cnm_p052"]},"nodeIds":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p159","cnm_p052","cnm_p050","cnm_p153"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p050"],"id":"list_totals","label":"Check the possible totals in order","methodDirectNodeIds":{"list_totals":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p052","cnm_p051"]},"nodeIds":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p052","cnm_p051","cnm_p050"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 25","reason":"Let the base-5 and base-6 units digits be r and s. The required equality modulo 5 gives s≡r, and modulo 2 excludes the exceptional pair r=0, s=5. Thus r=s≤4 and N=30k+r. The two tens digits are u=k mod 5 and v=(-k) mod 6. Matching modulo 100 gives u+v≡6k mod 10, leaving k≡0,1,12,13,24 mod 30. Three-digit solutions use k=12,13,24,30,31, each with five choices of r, for 25 choices.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_10B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","cnm_p159"],"id":"last-two-digits","label":"Track only the last two digits in each base","methodDirectNodeIds":{"last-two-digits":["cnm_024","math:simultaneous-congruences"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_024","math:simultaneous-congruences","math:modular-congruence","cnm_p159"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. 557","reason":"Let T be the number of teams and M=C(n,5). The averages are 126T/M and 56T/M, so M=84T. Divisibility holds exactly when n mod 16 is in {0,1,2,3,4,8,10,12}, n mod 9 is not in {5,8}, and n mod 7 is in {0,1,2,3,4}. Each period of 1008 contains 8*7*5=280 admissible residues. The interval 1 through 2016 contributes 560; removing 1,2,3,4 and adding 2017 gives 557.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"double-count-and-congruences","label":"Count incidences, then allowed residues","methodDirectNodeIds":{"double-count-and-congruences":["math:double-counting","cnm_h237","math:prime-factorization","math:simultaneous-congruences"]},"nodeIds":["math:double-counting","cnm_h237","math:prime-factorization","math:simultaneous-congruences"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 2519","reason":"N+1 must be divisible by every integer from 2 through 10. Their least common multiple is 2520, and 2519=2520-1 satisfies all the conditions.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1951_AMC_12_Q37","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p050","cnm_p153"],"id":"add-one-common-multiple","label":"Add one to make every division exact","methodDirectNodeIds":{"add-one-common-multiple":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p159","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p159","math:prime-factorization","cnm_p050","cnm_p153"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (27,720)","reason":"Every solution is 1+tL, where L=lcm(2,3,...,11)=2³·3²·5·7·11=27720. The two smallest solutions greater than 1 differ by L.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1978_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p050","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"],"id":"subtract-one-and-lcm","label":"Subtract 1 and find the common multiple","methodDirectNodeIds":{"subtract-one-and-lcm":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p159","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p159","math:prime-factorization","cnm_p050","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 25","reason":"Matching the units digits forces N=30k+j with 0≤j≤4 and 4≤k≤33. The tens-digit condition is (k mod 5)+((-k) mod 6)≡6k (mod 10), giving k≡0,1,12,13,24 (mod 30). In the allowed interval these are k=12,13,24,30,31. Each permits five values of j, so there are 25 choices.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","cnm_p159","cnm_p017","cnm_p050"],"id":"match-units-then-tens","label":"Match the units digits first, then the tens digits","methodDirectNodeIds":{"match-units-then-tens":["cnm_024","math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_h232"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_024","math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_h232","math:modular-congruence","cnm_p159","cnm_p017","cnm_p050"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 18, with n=1917","reason":"The conditions give n≡6 (mod 21), n not congruent to 6 (mod 63), and n≡117 (mod 120). The first and third imply n=237+840t. Above 1000, the first candidate is 1077, but gcd(63,1197)=63. The next is 1917, for which gcd(63,2037)=21 and gcd(1980,120)=60. Its digit sum is 1+9+1+7=18.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_10A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"combine-gcd-congruences","label":"Combine congruences and exclusions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"combine-gcd-congruences":["math:simultaneous-congruences"]},"nodeIds":["math:simultaneous-congruences"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"combine-exact-gcd-restrictions","label":"Find the candidate progression","methodDirectNodeIds":{"combine-exact-gcd-restrictions":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p158"]},"nodeIds":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_p158"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (5)","reason":"For exponents 2 through 6, the residues of powers of 6 modulo 100 are 36,16,96,76,56, and this five-step cycle repeats. Since 6^6 = 46656 ≡ 1 modulo 5, the last two digits are 56, giving tens digit 5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12B_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"remainders-modulo-four-and-twenty-five","label":"Combine two smaller remainders","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remainders-modulo-four-and-twenty-five":["math:modular-powers","math:simultaneous-congruences","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-powers","math:simultaneous-congruences","math:modular-congruence"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 3722","reason":"The first progression ends at 1 + 3·2003 = 6010. Common terms satisfy t ≡ 1 modulo 3 and t ≡ 2 modulo 7, so they are 16 + 21k. Within the shared range, k = 0 through 285, giving 286 common terms. Therefore the union has 4008 - 286 = 3722 elements.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_10B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"common-term-congruences","label":"Count common terms by congruences","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-term-congruences":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_h020"]},"nodeIds":["math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_h020"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 12","reason":"Let N = 90!/10²¹, since v5(90!) = 18 + 3 = 21. Also v2(90!) = 86, so N ≡ 0 modulo 4. Define U(m) as the product of integers up to m not divisible by 5. Modulo 25, U(90) = 1, U(18) = 4, and U(3) = 6. Thus N ≡ U(90)U(18)U(3)·2⁻²¹ ≡ 24·13 ≡ 12 modulo 25. Combining the two residues gives N ≡ 12 modulo 100.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_10A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"remove-zeros-and-combine-residues","label":"Remove zeros and combine residues","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remove-zeros-and-combine-residues":["math:simultaneous-congruences","math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:simultaneous-congruences","math:modular-powers"]},{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"],"id":"remove-zeros-and-find-remainders","label":"Remove the zeros and find small remainders","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remove-zeros-and-find-remainders":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","math:simultaneous-congruences"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","math:simultaneous-congruences","math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 12","reason":"The valuations are v₅(90!)=21 and v₂(90!)=86. Let U(N)=N!/5^{v₅(N!)}. Grouping factors not divisible by 5 gives U(90)≡1·4·6=24 modulo 25. Thus M=90!/10²¹ satisfies M≡24(2²¹)⁻¹≡12 modulo 25. Also v₂(M)=65, so M≡0 modulo 4. These conditions give M≡12 modulo 100.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"remove-zeros-and-combine-residues","label":"Remove zeros and combine residues","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remove-zeros-and-combine-residues":["math:simultaneous-congruences","math:modular-powers"]},"nodeIds":["math:simultaneous-congruences","math:modular-powers"]},{"contextNodeIds":["math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"],"id":"remove-fives-and-find-remainders","label":"Remove trailing zeros and track remainders","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remove-fives-and-find-remainders":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","math:simultaneous-congruences"]},"nodeIds":["math:prime-valuations-factorials","math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","math:simultaneous-congruences","math:factorial-notation","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 8","reason":"S(1)=2, S(2)=4, S(3)=8, and S(n)=S(n-1)+S(n-2)+S(n-3) for n≥4. The residue states repeat with period 4 modulo 4 and period 13 modulo 3. Thus S(2015)≡0 modulo 4 and ≡2 modulo 3, giving remainder 8 modulo 12.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_12A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h200","cnm_p153"],"id":"final-run-recurrence-residue-periods","label":"Build a recurrence and prove residue periods","methodDirectNodeIds":{"final-run-recurrence-residue-periods":["math:recursive-counting-probability","math:modular-congruence","math:simultaneous-congruences"]},"nodeIds":["math:recursive-counting-probability","math:modular-congruence","math:simultaneous-congruences","cnm_h200","cnm_p153"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"math:simultaneous-congruences","status":"mapped","total":18}
